ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain: the faces of one cylinder (or another
elementary surface of revolution) whose surfaces have reversed axes are
moved onto the reference face's parameters by the signed angle between
their X directions (mitcad#642).

Before merging faces on one surface, TransformPCurves gives the edges of
each face pcurves on the reference face's surface: it translates them
along the axis, mirrors them where the face's axis (v) or its frame's
handedness (u) is reversed against the reference's, and turns them by the
angle from the reference's X direction to the face's. Where neither is
reversed that angle is signed, in the sense the reference's u turns; where
either is, it was the unsigned angle between the two directions, so a face
whose X direction lay behind the reference's (the signed angle negative)
was turned the wrong way round, by twice that angle. A cut or an
intersection of a body by an extrusion going down gives such faces: the
tool's walls have their axes reversed against the body's (a bin's rounded
corner, its pieces split by a pattern of ribs, the faces of the corner
cylinder's axis pointing down in an indirect frame, 1.57 rad behind). The
moved pcurves then lay half a turn and more from where the faces are: the
edges between the faces got the two pcurves of a seam on the reference
surface, the faces' union looked as if it fell into separate intervals of
u, and each wire with such an edge was built as a face of its own on the
reference surface with the edge used once. The result was invalid
(BRepCheck_UnorientableShape), and the boolean operation's caller
computed it again without the merge (twice the time) and kept more than a
thousand faces of the body unmerged, on which a later rounding failed.

The angle is now always the signed one, from the reference's X direction
to the face's about the reference's X direction crossed with its Y
direction. Turning u by it and mirroring it as before puts each point of
the face where it lies on the reference surface: where only the axis is
reversed, u on the reference is u on the face plus the angle; where the
handedness is, the angle less u. Where neither is reversed nothing
changes, nor where the angle lies between 0 and pi.
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKShHealing/ShapeUpgrade/ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKShHealing/ShapeUpgrade/ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain.cxx
@@ -1445,19 +1445,12 @@
       Y_Reverse = true;
     }
 
-    if (!X_Reverse && !Y_Reverse)
-    {
-      gp_Dir DirRef = VdirRefSurf;
-      if (!AxisOfRefSurf.Direct())
-      {
-        DirRef.Reverse();
-      }
-      anAngle = XdirRefSurf.AngleWithRef(XdirSurfFace, DirRef);
-    }
-    else
-    {
-      anAngle = XdirRefSurf.Angle(XdirSurfFace);
-    }
+    // The angle from the reference's X direction to the face's, signed in
+    // the sense the reference's U parameter turns (CrossProd1), also where
+    // the face's axis or U parameter is reversed against the reference's:
+    // the unsigned angle turned such a face's pcurves half a turn or more
+    // the wrong way whenever its X direction lay behind the reference's.
+    anAngle = XdirRefSurf.AngleWithRef(XdirSurfFace, CrossProd1);
 
     ToRotate = (std::abs(anAngle) > Precision::PConfusion());
 
